#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157035 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "189d117b-b510-59dd-8340-a84bd1c08472" :question/math-academy-id "e-7409" :question/problem "The region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x+1)^2+y^2=1$ and the line $x=-1$, as shown above. Express the following double integral in polar coordinates:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2835/Source/Images/45342b77b0830e7179c7f7275385a285.png)\n\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D xy \\: \\textrm{d}A$" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$. The circle $(x+1)^2+y^2=1$ becomes $r^2+2r\\cos\\theta=0$, with outer boundary $r=-2\\cos\\theta$. The line $x=-1$ becomes $r=-\\sec\\theta$. The diagram shows the part above the $x$-axis to the left of this line; its angles run from the intersection $(-1,1)$ at $3\\pi/4$ to $\\pi$. Thus $$\\frac{3\\pi}4\\le\\theta\\le\\pi,\\qquad-\\sec\\theta\\le r\\le-2\\cos\\theta.$$ Since $xy\\,dA=r^3\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, $$\\iint_Dxy\\,dA=\\int_{3\\pi/4}^{\\pi}\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\int_{-\\sec\\theta}^{-2\\cos\\theta}r^3\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157036 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "859e1d71-21cb-5e30-9d60-a4b21358fe1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-7410" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{2y}{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A$, where the region $D$ is shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2835/Source/Images/a14cb4ba64dd6df3dde2fc1568d8d921.png)" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The circle $x^2+(y-1/2)^2=1/4$ becomes $r=\\sin\\theta$. The line $y=1/2$ becomes $r=\\tfrac12\\csc\\theta$. The diagram shows the first-quadrant part above the line, so $\\pi/4\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/2$ and $\\tfrac12\\csc\\theta\\le r\\le\\sin\\theta$. The transformed integrand including the area element is $(2r\\sin\\theta/r^2)r=2\\sin\\theta$. Therefore $$\\begin{aligned}I&=\\int_{\\pi/4}^{\\pi/2}\\int_{\\frac12\\csc\\theta}^{\\sin\\theta}2\\sin\\theta\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{\\pi/4}^{\\pi/2}(2\\sin^2\\theta-1)\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\left[-\\frac12\\sin(2\\theta)\\right]_{\\pi/4}^{\\pi/2}=\\frac12.\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157037 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f13c3a8-ba48-5299-b335-3752e8b92ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over an Annular Region to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac36f233-1f32-5cba-a35e-aa8e48207a18" :question/math-academy-id "e-7411" :question/problem "The region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between the quarter-circles of radii $3$ and $4$, as shown above. What is\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D \\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2835/Source/Images/dab6cca85da8e22d877207792771a513.png)" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 3 \\leq r \\leq 4, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\underset{D}{\\iint } \\frac{1}{{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}} \\, \\text{d} A & = \\underset{\\mathrm{\\Delta }}{\\iint } \\frac{1}{( r \\sin   \\theta {)}^{2} + ( r \\cos   \\theta {)}^{2}} \\, r \\, \\text{d} r \\text{d} \\theta \\\\  & = {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi  / 2} {\\int }_{3}^{4} \\frac{r}{{r}^{2} ( {\\sin }^{2}  \\theta + {\\cos }^{2}  \\theta )} \\, \\text{d} r \\, \\text{d} \\theta \\\\  & = {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi  / 2} {\\int }_{3}^{4} \\frac{1}{r} \\, \\text{d} r \\, \\text{d} \\theta  .\\end{array}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a1af7024-2d9e-5ffe-819c-acdf20bc6222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153858} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e3ea3c-1449-5822-91d5-ebfaa712eb8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153859} {:answer/id #uuid "26676f10-9bd7-509f-8ee0-3099f3bd2978" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153860} {:answer/id #uuid "33044875-6540-506f-8bf7-2e868a877e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153861} {:answer/id #uuid "5061f29d-83ee-5a8f-acfd-e15140b98ac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{72}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33044875-6540-506f-8bf7-2e868a877e2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153861} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f17e940-a965-5325-b128-00fa2d86e1a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2616ffa6-4ad5-55b3-bbe1-ee00aff863bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-161102" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2835/Source/Images/q-161102.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $\\underset{D}{\\iint } \\frac{1}{( {x}^{2} + {y}^{2} {)}^{2}} \\, \\text{d} A ,$, where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $2$ and $3$, as shown above." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The full annulus has $2\\le r\\le3$ and $0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$. The integrand $1/(x^2+y^2)^2$ becomes $r^{-4}$, and the area element adds $r$. Thus $$\\iint_D\\frac{dA}{(x^2+y^2)^2}=2\\pi\\int_2^3r^{-3}\\,dr=2\\pi\\left[-\\frac1{2r^2}\\right]_2^3=2\\pi\\left(\\frac18-\\frac1{18}\\right)=\\frac{5\\pi}{36}.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aad77980-4324-5d52-a93f-a37ab3390ef8" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over a Region Enclosed Between Two Curves to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bba07f2-4794-55af-a772-8be2f0978451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{- \\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\tan \\theta \\int _{2\\sec \\theta }^{4}rdrd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155921} {:answer/id #uuid "919e14e2-8417-5f72-a729-cd2d37a0937f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\tan \\theta \\int _{0}^{4}r^{2}drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155922} {:answer/id #uuid "2150c20a-648e-53fc-8cad-192160a17305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\tan \\theta \\int _{2\\sqrt{2}\\sec \\theta }^{4}rdrd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155923} {:answer/id #uuid "282f2098-8f08-5d6d-8e4a-68be5e9aa942" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\tan \\theta \\int _{\\sqrt{2}\\sec \\theta }^{4}rdrd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155924} {:answer/id #uuid "5c2cc414-abcd-507f-8635-ea1f78b3723a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{4}\\tan \\theta \\int _{2\\sec \\theta }^{4\\cos \\theta }rdrd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2150c20a-648e-53fc-8cad-192160a17305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\tan \\theta \\int _{2\\sqrt{2}\\sec \\theta }^{4}rdrd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155923} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e06f359-1f76-59b3-9610-e06ae6076faa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95f91c79-4c92-5753-8c02-6dfc6c0682b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-83980" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2835/Source/Images/q-83980.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $x^{2} + y^{2} = 16$ and the line $x = 2\\sqrt{2}$, as shown above. What is\n$\\iint _{D}\\frac{y}{x}dA$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows the part in the first quadrant to the right of $x=2\\sqrt2$ and inside $r=4$. The line becomes $r=2\\sqrt2\\sec\\theta$, meeting the circle at angle $\\pi/4$. Thus $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/4$ and $2\\sqrt2\\sec\\theta\\le r\\le4$. Since $y/x=\\tan\\theta$ and $dA=r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, the integral is $$\\int_0^{\\pi/4}\\tan\\theta\\int_{2\\sqrt2\\sec\\theta}^4r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f13c3a8-ba48-5299-b335-3752e8b92ef4" :knowledge-point/title "Converting an Integral Over an Annular Region to Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dcb979f-3921-508f-9b2b-ae8e7145b9e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi }\\int _{1}^{2}r(\\cos \\theta  - \\sin \\theta )drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155975} {:answer/id #uuid "0eef47cb-4788-507e-b1f4-c3795d6ae85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi }\\int _{1}^{2}r^{2}(\\cos \\theta  - \\sin \\theta )drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155976} {:answer/id #uuid "2033d184-d234-5f04-a79e-49eafcb830da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi /2}\\int _{0}^{2}r^{2}(\\sin \\theta  - \\cos \\theta )drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155977} {:answer/id #uuid "2274961c-375d-58ac-94ad-7773f6f452f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{1}^{2}\\int _{0}^{\\pi /2}r^{3}(\\cos \\theta  - \\sin \\theta )drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155978} {:answer/id #uuid "751a9151-32fb-5553-b22a-f8cb9eecd5c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi }\\int _{1}^{4}r(\\cos \\theta  - \\sin \\theta )drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0eef47cb-4788-507e-b1f4-c3795d6ae85a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int _{0}^{\\pi }\\int _{1}^{2}r^{2}(\\cos \\theta  - \\sin \\theta )drd\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155976} :answer-field/id #uuid "94ce5ea4-7b87-505a-8ba6-b0fd5e7fa28d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97fc9bc2-d10b-5e4f-be32-39965512311e" :question/math-academy-id "q-85059" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2835/Source/Images/q-85059.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the upper-half plane is enclosed between two semi-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $2$, as shown above. What is\n$\\iint _{D}(x - y)dA$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The upper half-annulus has $1\\le r\\le2$ and $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi$. The integrand becomes $x-y=r(\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta)$, and the area element adds another factor $r$. Therefore $$\\iint_D(x-y)\\,dA=\\int_0^\\pi\\int_1^2r^2(\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.$$"}]}
